          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long should a degreaser sit on concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most labels call for about five to ten minutes of dwell time before scrubbing and rinsing. The key is not to let it dry. On a hot or sunny day work in small sections and mist the area with water. Microbial products are the exception: they are left on for days and reapplied over weeks."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I degrease concrete before sealing or epoxy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Any oil left in the surface stops sealers penetrating and stops epoxy bonding, which is a common cause of peeling. Degrease the whole slab, rinse thoroughly, let it dry completely, then follow the coating maker's prep, which for epoxy usually includes etching or grinding. Spot-test old oil areas with a few drops of water to check they absorb."
          }
