          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A concrete degreaser is a cleaner, not a miracle. Mixed at the right strength and scrubbed in, a good alkaline concentrate lifts the grease film and fresh drips that make a garage floor look tired. It will not pull a five-year-old black stain out of the slab, and nothing sold by the gallon will. Buy for the job in front of you: the whole floor, a driveway before sealing, or a pressure washer tank.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What a Concrete Degreaser Does, and What It Does Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete is a sponge with very small holes. Motor oil, transmission fluid and grease soak into those pores within minutes, and once they are below the surface no amount of scrubbing on top reaches them. A degreaser works on what it can touch: it surrounds oil molecules with surfactants, or dissolves them with a solvent, so that water can carry them away. That makes it excellent at the oily film on a shop floor, the grey tire tracks in a garage, fresh drips and the general grime that builds up on a driveway. It makes it much weaker against oil that has already wicked a quarter inch down.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the line between this page and our guide to the <a href="/best-oil-stain-remover-for-concrete/">best oil stain removers for concrete</a>. Stain removers are mostly poultices and microbial powders designed to draw or digest oil out of the pores over hours or weeks. Degreasers are wash products: you mix them, apply them, agitate and rinse, and the job is done in an afternoon. Most garages need both at some point, but they solve different problems and they are bought by the gallon for different reasons.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are here because of a single dark spot under the engine, read <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">how to get oil out of concrete</a> first. It covers the absorbent, poultice and microbial methods that actually reach deep oil, and it is honest about how clean old concrete can get. A degreaser is part of that process, usually as the first wash and the final rinse, but it is rarely the whole answer to an old stain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where degreasers earn their place is everything else. Prepping a slab before a sealer or an epoxy coat, where any surface oil will cause the coating to fail. Cleaning a whole two-car floor that has gone grey with years of tire grime. Running through a pressure washer on a driveway before the house goes on the market, a job our guide to the <a href="/best-driveway-cleaner/">best driveway cleaners</a> covers for mildew, rust and tire marks as well as oil. Washing down a workshop floor where a parts washer drips. These are surface jobs measured in hundreds of square feet, and concentrate economics matter: a jug that makes 10 to 20 gallons of working solution is a very different buy from a 32 ounce spray bottle.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Degreasers clean surfaces. Poultices and microbes treat stains.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Use a degreaser for whole-floor cleaning, coating prep and fresh oil. For a stain that has been there for months, a degreaser is the first step, not the fix.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The 6 Best Concrete Degreasers
          </h2>

          <p>
            We chose these on what can be checked: the chemistry each maker discloses, the dilution rates printed on the label or the maker's product page, the surfaces each is sold for, pack size, and whether it is built for a bucket and brush, a pressure washer, or both. We did not run our own floor tests. Where retailers and the label disagree on a mix rate, we say so and point you to the jug, because the label on the bottle you buy is the one that counts.
