        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Alkaline, Solvent, Enzyme and Microbial: The Four Chemistries
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every concrete degreaser on the shelf falls into one of four families, and knowing which one you are holding tells you more than the marketing on the front of the jug. The families differ in speed, smell, what surfaces they are safe on, and whether they clean the surface or work their way down into the slab.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water-based alkaline</h3>

          <p>
            This is the workhorse category: Oil Eater Original, Zep Driveway and Concrete Cleaner, Simple Green Pro HD and the Krud Kutter pressure washer concentrate all sit here. They combine surfactants with alkaline builders that saponify and emulsify oil, turning it into something water can rinse away. They are non-flammable, mix with tap water, and are cheap per gallon of solution. Concrete is itself strongly alkaline, so a high pH cleaner does not attack it the way an acid does. Their weakness is time: they need several minutes of dwell and real scrubbing, and they slow down sharply on cold slabs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Solvent and citrus</h3>

          <p>
            Solvent degreasers dissolve oil rather than emulsifying it. On concrete the common choice is a citrus formula built on d-limonene, the oil from orange peel, such as Zep Heavy-Duty Citrus Degreaser. Solvents cut thick grease, tar and adhesive residue faster than water-based cleaners, which is why they are popular for sticky buildup around workbenches and under lawn equipment. The trade-offs are a strong smell, the need for ventilation in a closed garage, and a real risk to asphalt, rubber and some sealers, which solvents can soften.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Enzyme cleaners</h3>

          <p>
            Enzyme products use proteins that break fats and oils into smaller pieces. They are common in kitchen and food-plant floor cleaners and work best on cooking grease and organic soil. On motor oil, which is a petroleum product rather than a fat, they are less convincing on their own, and most of the enzyme claims you see on concrete products are paired with surfactants that do the immediate cleaning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Microbial or bioremediation</h3>

          <p>
            Bioremediation products such as Terminator-HSD contain cultured microbes that consume hydrocarbons and break them down into carbon dioxide and water. This is the only chemistry that can follow oil down into the slab, because the microbes keep working as long as there is moisture and oil to eat. It is also the slowest by far: the maker and sellers describe repeat applications over weeks, and owners report mixed results. Treat it as a stain treatment that happens to come in the degreaser aisle.
          </p>

