                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Simple Green Pro HD</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1:10</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1:3</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Do not use over asphalt; do not let dry</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oil Eater Original</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1:10</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Down to 1:1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Full chart on the jug's rear label</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Krud Kutter 385466</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Per label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Per label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Retailers list up to 20 gallons per jug</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Three habits make any ratio work better. First, use hot water where the label allows it. Oil softens with heat and surfactants work faster, which is why Zep specifies hot water on its concrete cleaner. Second, pre-wet the slab with plain water before applying the mix on a hot day, so the concrete does not soak up the degreaser before it reaches the oil. Third, mix in a bucket rather than pouring concentrate onto the floor and adding water after; neat concentrate on a dry slab is harder to rinse and more likely to leave a light patch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start in the middle of the range and adjust. If the mix foams and looks clean after scrubbing but the floor stays grey when it dries, go stronger. If the floor dries with a dull, slightly sticky film, you went too strong or did not rinse enough, and a plain-water rinse will usually fix it. Write down the ratio that worked on your floor; it will be the same next spring.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fresh-vs-set" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Fresh Oil vs Set-In Oil
          </h2>

          <p>
            A drip that landed this morning and a stain that has been under the car for three winters are different problems, and the degreaser you choose should change with them. Fresh oil is mostly on the surface and in the top layer of pores. Set-in oil has wicked down, partly oxidised and bonded with dirt, and what you see is a stain in the concrete rather than a film on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For fresh oil, the order is absorbent first, degreaser second. Cover the puddle with clay, diatomaceous earth or a pad, as covered in our guide to the <a href="/best-oil-absorbent/">best oil absorbents</a>, and let it pull up everything it can. Sweep it up, then scrub the shadow with an alkaline degreaser at the heavy-soil ratio, let it dwell for five to ten minutes, and rinse. Done within a day, this routine usually leaves little or no mark on unsealed concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            For set-in oil, a degreaser is the opening move. A strong alkaline wash removes the dirt and surface oil that sit on top of the stain, which often lightens it noticeably and shows you how much is actually in the concrete. Repeat once. If the mark is still there, switch tools: a poultice that draws oil up as it dries, or a microbial powder left to work for weeks. Our step-by-step guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains from a driveway</a> walks through those methods in order of effort.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is one trap here. Repeated aggressive scrubbing with a strong solvent can spread a set-in stain, because it re-dissolves the oil and carries it outward into clean concrete. If the stain is getting wider and paler rather than smaller, stop scrubbing and let an absorbent or poultice pull the loosened oil up instead. Water-based alkaline cleaners are less prone to this than solvents, which is another reason they are the default for concrete.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">RULE OF THUMB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Under a day old: absorb, then degrease. Over a month old: degrease, then poultice or microbes.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The degreaser is in both routines. What changes is whether it is the finish or the start.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="pressure-washer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Using a Degreaser With a Pressure Washer
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pressure washer is the fastest way to rinse a big slab, and with the right concentrate it is a fast way to clean one too. The important thing to understand is that the machine applies detergent at low pressure and rinses at high pressure; it does not blast detergent into the concrete. Most consumer machines draw detergent through an onboard tank or a siphon tube only when the wide, low-pressure soap nozzle is fitted, and they dilute it heavily as they go.
          </p>

          <p>
            That dilution is why some degreasers are sold as pressure washer concentrates. The Krud Kutter Concrete and Driveway Pressure Washer Concentrate is formulated to be drawn through the machine and is also labeled for manual use, so the same jug works with a pump sprayer on a small area. General-purpose concentrates like Simple Green Pro HD tell you to pre-mix according to your equipment instructions or drop the siphon hose straight in. Check your washer's manual before you put any solvent or citrus product through it; seals and pumps are not always rated for them.
          </p>

          <p>
            The working sequence is simple. Pre-rinse to wet the slab and knock off loose dirt. Apply the detergent with the soap nozzle, working from the low end of the driveway to the high end so it does not streak. Let it dwell for the time on the label, typically five to ten minutes, without letting it dry; mist it with water if the sun is on it. Scrub the oil spots with a stiff brush while the rest dwells. Then rinse with a 25 or 15 degree nozzle or a surface cleaner, working from the high end down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pressure does not replace chemistry on oil. A 3,000 PSI blast will clean the grey off concrete and lighten a fresh drip, but on a set-in stain it mostly removes the dirt around it and can etch a visible clean patch that makes the stain look worse. Our guide to the <a href="/best-pressure-washer-for-concrete/">best pressure washers for concrete</a> covers PSI, flow and surface cleaners, and the short answer is that flow rate and a surface cleaner do more for a driveway than raw PSI.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, a pressure washer moves a lot of dirty water fast. Everything you rinse off goes somewhere, and on a sloped driveway that is usually the street gutter. That matters more than most people think, and it is covered in the runoff section below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surfaces" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Garage Floors, Driveways and Coated Concrete
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bare, unsealed concrete is the easy case. It tolerates alkaline degreasers at full label strength, it tolerates scrubbing with a stiff nylon brush, and it rinses clean. Most of the problems people have with degreasers come from using them on something that is not bare concrete: a sealed or coated floor, an asphalt driveway, or pavers with polymeric sand in the joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealed concrete is the most common. Acrylic sealers sit on the surface as a thin film, and solvents, including citrus, can soften or cloud them. Penetrating silane and siloxane sealers live inside the pores and are much more tolerant, though a strong degreaser can still reduce their water beading over time. Start with a mild alkaline mix on any sealed slab, test it on a hidden corner, and avoid citrus and solvent products unless the sealer maker approves them. If you are about to seal, our guide to the <a href="/best-penetrating-concrete-sealer/">best penetrating concrete sealers</a> explains why the slab must be oil-free first.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxy and polyaspartic garage floors need even more restraint. The coating is non-porous, so oil sits on top and wipes away with a mild cleaner; there is no reason to use a heavy degreaser, and harsh chemicals can dull the topcoat. If you are preparing bare concrete for a coating, though, degreasing is mandatory: any oil left in the surface will stop epoxy bonding and cause peeling. Our <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">garage floor epoxy guide</a> covers prep, and most kit makers specify a degrease, rinse and etch or grind sequence.
          </p>

          <p>
            Asphalt driveways are a different material entirely. Asphalt is bound with petroleum, so oil and solvent degreasers attack the binder itself. Simple Green states plainly that Pro HD should not be used over asphalt. Zep and Krud Kutter label their concrete products for asphalt as well, but at the lighter end of the mix range and with prompt rinsing. On asphalt, absorb fresh oil fast and use the mildest product that works.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pavers sit between the two. The stone or concrete units handle an alkaline degreaser, but repeated high-pressure rinsing will wash polymeric sand out of the joints. Use a fan nozzle at a shallow angle and plan to top up the joint sand afterwards if you clean the whole patio.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runoff" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Etching, Runoff and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the myth: a proper concrete degreaser does not etch concrete. Etching is what acids do, by dissolving the cement paste at the surface, and it is how muriatic acid and phosphoric etchers prepare a slab for coating. The products on this page are alkaline or neutral-to-mildly-alkaline solvents, and concrete is itself highly alkaline. What people mistake for etching is usually a clean patch: the degreaser and brush removed years of dirt from one spot, so it is lighter than the floor around it. The fix is to clean the whole bay, not the spot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Runoff is the bigger issue, and it is the one most guides skip. Storm drains in most US towns carry water untreated to creeks and rivers, and many local codes prohibit washing oil, detergent or wash water into them. A driveway degreasing job sends both oil and surfactant down the gutter. The responsible approach is to absorb as much free oil as you can before washing, block the nearest drain with a sock or a sandbag, and either let wash water soak into a lawn or gravel area or pick it up with a wet vac for disposal down a sink drain to the sanitary sewer, where your local rules allow.
          </p>

          <p>
            Biodegradable on a label is not permission to send it to the creek. It means the surfactants break down over time in the right conditions, not that the oil they are carrying does. The oil is the pollutant; the degreaser just made it mobile. That is also why absorbing first matters: every ounce caught in clay or a pad is an ounce that never reaches the gutter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Personal safety is straightforward but worth stating. Alkaline concentrates are skin and eye irritants, so wear nitrile gloves and safety glasses while mixing and scrubbing. Zep's safety data sheet for its concrete cleaner lists 2-butoxyethanol, a glycol ether solvent common in heavy-duty cleaners, which is another reason to ventilate an enclosed garage. Citrus degreasers are strong-smelling and, while far less volatile than gasoline, should not be used near a running water heater pilot in a closed space. Never mix a degreaser with bleach or an acid cleaner.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">BEFORE YOU RINSE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Absorb, block the drain, then wash.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Pick up free oil first, put a sock or sandbag across the nearest storm drain, and send rinse water to grass, gravel or a wet vac, not the gutter.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="method" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. A Step-by-Step Degreasing Method for a Garage Floor
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the routine for a typical two-car garage floor that has gone grey with tire grime and has a few drip marks. It assumes bare or penetrating-sealed concrete and a water-based alkaline concentrate. Budget about two hours plus drying time, and pick a day above 50 degrees Fahrenheit, since cold slabs slow the chemistry and take a long time to dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            First, clear the floor and sweep it thoroughly. Grit turns a scrub brush into sandpaper and soaks up degreaser you meant for the oil. Treat any fresh, wet oil with an absorbent and sweep it up. Then wet the whole floor with plain water from a hose or a pump sprayer, which stops the concrete drinking the cleaner and evens out the result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, mix the degreaser in a bucket at the light-soil ratio for the whole floor, and keep a smaller container at the heavy-soil ratio for the drip zones. Apply the light mix with a pump sprayer or by pouring and spreading with a push broom, working one bay at a time so nothing dries. Put the strong mix on the oil marks. Let everything dwell for the label time, usually five to ten minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, scrub. A stiff nylon deck brush on a long handle does the floor; a hand brush does the drip spots. Work in overlapping passes. You should see the solution go brown or grey; that is the dirt and oil coming out. On a dirty floor a rotary floor scrubber or a pressure washer surface cleaner saves real effort, and both can be rented.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fourth, rinse thoroughly with clean water, working toward the door, and pick up the dirty water with a squeegee and a wet vac rather than pushing it onto the driveway. Our guide to the <a href="/best-shop-vac/">best shop vacs</a> covers the sizes that make this quick. Rinse a second time if the floor feels slick. Let it dry completely, ideally overnight, before you judge the result. Any marks that remain once the floor is dry are in the concrete, not on it, and that is the point to switch to a poultice or a microbial treatment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating an old stain with endless scrubbing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A degreaser lifts surface oil. If a stain has not changed after two strong washes, it is in the concrete, and more scrubbing can spread it outward. Switch to a poultice or microbial treatment rather than burning through another gallon of concentrate.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing every job at the same strength</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Zep, Simple Green and Oil Eater all publish a range of ratios for a reason. Use the light ratio for the whole floor and the heavy ratio only on drip zones. Too strong leaves a residue that attracts dirt; too weak runs out before the oil does.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the solution dry on the slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Degreasers work while they are wet. On a sunny driveway a mix can dry in minutes and leave a film. Work in smaller sections, pre-wet the concrete, and mist with water during the dwell time if it starts to dry.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using solvents on asphalt or sealed concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Citrus and solvent degreasers can soften asphalt binder and acrylic sealers, and Simple Green says Pro HD should not be used over asphalt. On blacktop or a coated floor, use the mildest product that works and test a hidden spot first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Washing oil and detergent into the storm drain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Storm drains usually run untreated to local waterways. Absorb free oil before washing, block the nearest drain, and send rinse water to grass or gravel or pick it up with a wet vac. Biodegradable surfactants do not make the oil safe.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

