        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best concrete degreaser?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most garages, Oil Eater Original 1 Gallon is the best all-round concrete degreaser: water-based, non-flammable and diluted to suit anything from a whole-floor wash to a fresh oil drip. For a whole driveway, Zep ZUCON128 is cheaper per gallon of solution, and for pressure washers the Krud Kutter 385466 concentrate is made to be drawn through the machine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a degreaser remove old oil stains from concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not completely. A degreaser cleans oil on and just below the surface, so it lightens an old stain by removing the dirt and surface oil on top of it. Oil that has wicked deep into the slab needs a poultice that draws it out as it dries, or a microbial product that digests it over several weeks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a degreaser in my pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the product is labeled for it or the maker gives pressure washer instructions. Krud Kutter 385466 is formulated for pressure washers, and Simple Green gives pre-mix and siphon directions for Pro HD. Check your washer's manual before running citrus or solvent products through the pump, and always apply detergent with the low-pressure soap nozzle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does degreaser damage concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Alkaline degreasers do not etch concrete, which is itself strongly alkaline; etching is done by acids. A cleaned spot can look lighter than the surrounding floor because it is simply cleaner. Solvent degreasers can, however, soften asphalt and some acrylic sealers, so test on sealed or coated surfaces before using them broadly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Oil Eater safe for asphalt driveways?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Oil Eater is water-based and listings describe it as free of petroleum solvents, which makes it gentler on asphalt than citrus or solvent products. Use a lighter ratio on blacktop, do not let it sit long or dry, and rinse thoroughly. For fresh oil on asphalt, absorbing the spill quickly matters more than which degreaser you choose.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a degreaser sit on concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most labels call for about five to ten minutes of dwell time before scrubbing and rinsing. The key is not to let it dry. On a hot or sunny day work in small sections and mist the area with water. Microbial products are the exception: they are left on for days and reapplied over weeks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I degrease concrete before sealing or epoxy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Any oil left in the surface stops sealers penetrating and stops epoxy bonding, which is a common cause of peeling. Degrease the whole slab, rinse thoroughly, let it dry completely, then follow the coating maker's prep, which for epoxy usually includes etching or grinding. Spot-test old oil areas with a few drops of water to check they absorb.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

